2026-09-16T11:25Z claude note Filed from the round-1 review panel on PR #348 (ticket 0283). 0283 made a stampless diverged store visible on erg check and erg update; every defect below is about what erg init then does with it, which is why they are one ticket and not three. Defects 1 and 2 were each reproduced end to end by two independent reviewers; defect 3 by one, with a stated repro. All three are pre-existing code -- none is a regression from 0283 -- but 0283 is what starts steering users into defect 1, which is why it is the blocking one.

--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0283 fixed the root-cause silence: `assetDriftWarnings` now reports an
asset that differs from the embedded copy when no `.erg-assets` manifest
exists. Two of its three channels work. Everything that is still broken lives
on the `erg init` side, and the review panel on PR #348 found three defects
there that share one shape -- **init treats a store with no provenance as a
store whose provenance it may invent.**

They are filed together because they are one logical unit: whoever opens
`init.go` for any of them will be reading the same twenty lines.

## Defect 1 (blocking, and the reason this ticket exists now)

**`erg init` stamps a preserved local edit with the EMBEDDED hash, silencing
the condition permanently.**

`buildManifest()` always hashes `bootstrapAsset(rel)` -- the content this
binary ships -- never the bytes on disk. `installAssets` calls `writeManifest`
even for a file it just preserved because of local edits; only the *rollback*-
preserved case is exempt (`init.go:281`, `if rollbackPreserved { return ... }`).

So on exactly the scenario 0283 was filed for -- a customised `.ergrc`, no
manifest -- `erg init` correctly preserves the customisation and then writes a
manifest stamping that file with the embedded hash. From the next run on, the
stamped branch compares embedded-hash against current-embedded-hash, finds them
equal, and reports nothing. The divergence is still on disk, now permanently
invisible and recorded as something it is not:

```
erg check  -> NOTE .ergrc: no .erg-assets stamp -- ...
erg init   -> init: tickets/.ergrc has local edits -- preserving ...
erg check  -> ERG CHECK: PASS (1 tickets)          # silent, permanently
```

The rationale for the fix is already written, in this repo, three lines above
the offending call: the `rollbackPreserved` exemption's own comment says
stamping a preserved file "would destroy the very evidence that said so, making
the next run misreport the same rollback as a local edit." That was written for
ticket 0279 and applies identically here; it was simply never extended.

Because of this, 0283 deliberately does NOT tell the user to run `erg init` to
resolve the condition -- `assetStamplessSignal` says to compare against the
shipped copy first, and `update.go`'s follow-up line sends the reader to
`erg check`. **Both wordings should be revisited when this ticket lands**, and
`assetStamplessSignal` is a cross-version literal by then, so it may be
extended at the END only, never rewritten.

## Defect 2 — `erg init`'s chained check is unreachable, so channel 2 of 0283's
exit criterion 1 is unmet

0283's exit criterion names three channels: `erg check`, `erg init`'s chained
post-init check, and `erg update`'s post-swap hint. The chained check cannot
fire for ANY asset warning, before or after 0283:

- `init.go` returns 2 at `if skipped > 0` on **both** the dry-run and the real
  leg, several lines above the chained `corpusWarnings` block. A stampless
  store with a diverged asset always skips, so it always takes the early return.
- On the non-skipping leg, `installAssets` has already written the manifest, so
  the store is no longer stampless by the time the chained check runs.

Independently verified by three reviewers. The observable consequence is 0283's
own motivating reproduction, unchanged: `erg init -n` prints
`would preserve (local edits)` with no warning anywhere.

Related and worth fixing in the same pass: `init.go:212` flatly asserts
`has local edits` for a preserved file. With no stamp there is no basis for that
claim -- it is precisely the attribution `assetStamplessSignal` exists to
refuse, and the same false-reason class ticket 0279 already fixed on the
rollback leg.

Two candidate shapes, and choosing between them is the architectural decision
this ticket carries:

- move the chained check above the early return (changes init's output ordering
  for every exit-2 run), or
- give `installAssets` a third preserve reason -- "differs, no stamp, reason
  unknown" -- alongside the existing local-edit and rollback ones, and let the
  per-file line carry it.

The second is closer to what 0279 established and does not disturb exit
ordering; the first is what the exit criterion's literal wording asks for.

## Defect 3 — a PARTIAL manifest reproduces the identical silence 0283 closed

`assetDriftWarnings`' stamped branch skips any asset with no entry
(`if !ok || stamp == "" { continue }`) and does not fall back to
`stamplessWarnings`; `parseManifest` returns non-nil as soon as *one* line
parses. So a manifest that stamps `.ergrc` but not `AGENTS.md` silences
AGENTS.md divergence completely -- not drift-warned (no stamp for it), not
stampless-warned (the manifest is not nil).

Repro: `erg init`, delete the `AGENTS.md` line from `.erg-assets`, diverge
`AGENTS.md` on disk, `erg check` -> `PASS`, zero warnings.

Same defect class as 0283's, one level down: the gate is "a manifest exists"
where it should be "this asset is stamped". The fix is per-asset rather than
per-store -- route an unstamped asset through the stampless path even when the
manifest is present.

## Defect 4 — no way to see the embedded copy

Found by the round-2 red team on PR #348. 0283's message tells the reader the
on-disk asset differs from what the binary ships, and **no `erg` subcommand can
show them what it ships.** The full surface was enumerated: `erg init -n`
reports only *that* a file differs, `erg spec` and `erg integration` dump
different embedded files entirely, and nothing exposes `bootstrapAsset(".ergrc")`.
The only route today is a workaround nobody would find: a second store, a copy
of the binary, `erg init`, then a manual `diff`.

0283 therefore points the reader at the store's git history instead, which is
honest but is not erg answering for itself. Exposing the embedded copy — a
`--show`/`--diff` flag on `erg init`, or an `erg spec`-style dump — is what
would let that message name an erg command, and it is the prerequisite for any
"compare them yourself" advice anywhere in the tool.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/init.go` — `installAssets` (the `writeManifest` call and the
  `rollbackPreserved` exemption above it), the preserve-reason strings around
  line 212, and `cmdInit`'s `if skipped > 0 { return 2 }` on both legs
- `src/go/manifest.go` — `buildManifest` (hashes embedded, not disk),
  `assetDriftWarnings`' per-asset skip, `stamplessWarnings`,
  `assetStamplessSignal`
- `src/go/manifest_test.go` — `TestAssetDriftWarningsReportsStamplessDivergence`
  and the `stamplessFixture` helper are the idiom to extend
- `tests/test_check.sh` — the `stampless` / `stampmatch` fixtures
- `tests/test_init.sh` — where defect 2's channel assertion belongs

## Actions

Ordered so the state-corrupting one lands first and nothing depends on a
decision not yet made.

1. **Stop stamping what init did not touch (defect 1).** Extend `init.go`'s
   `rollbackPreserved` exemption to cover the local-edits-preserved case: a
   file `installAssets` preserved does not get an entry in the manifest it
   writes. The exemption's existing comment already states the rationale;
   generalise it rather than adding a second special case beside it.
2. **Decide the channel-2 shape (defect 2), then implement it.** Either move
   `cmdInit`'s chained check above `if skipped > 0 { return 2 }` on both legs,
   or give `installAssets` a third preserve reason ("differs, no stamp, reason
   unknown") carried on the per-file line. The second is closer to what 0279
   established and does not disturb exit ordering; the first is what 0283's
   original criterion asked for literally. This is the architectural call --
   settle it in the ticket log before writing code.
3. **Fix the false reason (defect 2, second half).** `init.go:212`'s flat
   `has local edits` must not be printed for a file whose provenance the code
   never observed.
4. **Gate per asset, not per store (defect 3).** Route an asset with no entry
   in an otherwise-valid manifest through `stamplessWarnings` instead of
   skipping it.
5. **Expose the embedded copy (defect 4).** Pick one surface and document it in
   the relevant `help*` constant; regenerate `docs/erg-manual.md`.
6. **Revisit 0283's wordings.** `assetStamplessSignal` can name an erg command
   once action 5 lands, and `update.go`'s follow-up line can stop routing
   through `erg check`. Both are cross-version literals by then: extend at the
   END only, never rewrite.

## Test

Red step first, and it must assert on state AFTER the recommended action --
that is the whole gap 0283's suite has, where every arm asserts the state
immediately following `check` or `update`:

- `TestInitDoesNotStampAPreservedLocalEdit` — stampless fixture with a diverged
  `.ergrc`, run `installAssets` with `refuseDiverged=true`, then assert the
  written manifest has NO entry for `.ergrc` (or none at all), and that a
  following `assetDriftWarnings` still reports the divergence. Today the second
  assertion fails: the condition goes silent.
- Positive control in the same test: a clean-upgrade or unchanged asset IS
  stamped, so the fix does not simply stop writing manifests.
- `tests/test_check.sh` — partial-manifest fixture for defect 3, with the
  matching-content sibling as its silent control.
- `tests/test_init.sh` — defect 2's channel: a stampless diverged store must
  report its condition through `erg init` (and through `erg init -n`), not only
  through `erg check`.

## Verification

- [ ] Run `erg check` on a stampless diverged store, then `erg init`, then
      `erg check` again: the second check still reports the condition. Today
      it is silent, which is defect 1's whole signature.
- [ ] `grep` the manifest `erg init` wrote: no entry for the preserved file.
- [ ] `erg init` and `erg init -n` on that store both report the condition
      (defect 2), and neither claims `has local edits` for a file whose
      provenance was never observed.
- [ ] Delete one asset's line from a valid `.erg-assets`, diverge that asset,
      `erg check` reports it (defect 3). Sibling control: restore the line,
      it goes quiet.
- [ ] The command chosen in action 5 prints the embedded copy byte-identical
      to `bootstrapAsset`, verified by piping it through `sha256sum` against
      the manifest a clean `erg init` writes.
- [ ] `make docs` leaves no drift; `make check` passes.

## Invariants

- No change to the overwrite/preserve DECISION. All three defects are about
  what is recorded and what is said, never about which bytes win.
- `erg init` on a pristine current asset stays a silent no-op.
- `assetDriftSignal` and `assetRollbackSignal` are cross-version literals:
  extend at the END only. `assetStamplessSignal` joins them once 0283 ships.
- A store that never adopted erg's asset management is still never nagged.

## Exit criteria

- A locally-edited asset preserved by `erg init` is not stamped as if shipped,
  and the condition remains reportable on the next `erg check`
- `erg init` (and `erg init -n`) reports a stampless store's condition, closing
  channel 2 of ticket 0283's exit criterion 1
- A preserved file's stated reason is one the code can justify from what it
  observed
- An asset missing from an otherwise-valid manifest is reported, not silently
  skipped
- `make check` passes

## Related

- 0283 — made the condition visible on `erg check` and `erg update`; this
  ticket is the `erg init` half, filed from PR #348's review panel
- 0279 — established "don't stamp what you didn't touch" for the rollback case;
  defect 1 is that rule not extended to the local-edit case
- 0281 — the historical hash table, closed wontfix. Out of scope here: none of
  these defects need one, and a wrong entry in such a table destroys a genuine
  local edit
- 0278 — tracker for the asset-machinery defect class
